  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{-5}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=5{x}^{-4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y={x}^{-4}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{x}^{-5}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
